Assuming the answer you want is "John John", the best workaround right now is

```C#
AsnReader outer = new AsnReader(rawDataBytes, AsnEncodingRules.DER);
AsnReader sequence = outer.ReadSequence();
outer.ThrowIfNotEmpty();

while (sequence.HasData)
{
    // Windows allows this set to be improperly sorted, and we've seen it in the wild.
    AsnReader setOf = sequence.ReadSetOf(skipSortOrderValidation: true);

    while (setOf.HasData)
    {
        AsnReader rdn = setOf.ReadSequence();
        string oid = rdn.ReadObjectIdentifier();

        if (oid == "2.5.4.3")
        {
            Asn1Tag tag = rdn.PeekTag();

            if (tag.TagClass != TagClass.Universal)
            {
                throw new AsnContentException();
            }

            return reader.ReadCharacterString((UniversalTagNumber)tag.TagValue);
        }
    }
}

return null;
```

Making use of the new System.Formats.Asn1 package.

@bartonjs I'm waffling on the property vs. method for `GetSingleValueValue`.

Consider code like this:

```C#
foreach (X500RelativeDistinguishedName rdn in dn.EnumerateRelativeDistinguishedNames())
{
    if (!rdn.HasMultipleValues)
    {
        System.Console.WriteLine(rdn.SingleValueType.Value);
    }
}
```

This will produce a `nullable` warning on `SingleValueType` because it may be null, and null-ref accessing `Value`.

But wait! We can annotate `HasMultipleValues` to give the compiler a hint about nullability.

```C#
[MemberNotNullWhen(false, nameof(SingleValueType))]
public bool HasMultipleValues { get; }
```

Tada. No more compiler warning.

Oh. `MemberNotNullWhen` does not work on methods. This does not work (and the compiler tells me as such):

```C#
[MemberNotNullWhen(false, nameof(GetSingleValueValue))]
public bool HasMultipleValues { get; }
```

If it were a property, then we could give better nullability hints. On the other hand, I'm not sure it's worth bending the API shape to support better nullable annotations.

Overall I think the API shape is fine. `HasMultipleValues` should definitely be annotated for `SingleValueType`'s nullability. It would be nice if this worked for `GetSingleValueValue`, but we can't unless it's a property (or field, but, yeah).

[Video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PJh2esB0Efw&t=1h45m16s)

* Changed "Value" to "Element"
* Made SingleElementType a method instead of a null-returning property

```C#
